The *Import documents* wizard brings writing you did not create in Skribisto into a project. A
file you sent out for someone to mark up comes back through [the round trip](:help-round-trip).
A whole project made in another application arrives through
[bringing a whole project across](:help-import-projects).

## Opening the wizard

Choose *Work ▸ Import from ▸ Documents (Markdown, Word, ODT)…*. To open it with a destination
already chosen, right-click an item in the binder and choose *Import here…*.

Nothing is written to your project until you press *Import* on the last step.

## Choosing the files

Drop files onto the *Files* page, or press *Browse…* to pick them. These formats are read:

- Markdown (`.md`)
- Word (`.docx`)
- OpenDocument (`.odt`)
- plain text (`.txt`)

Reorder or remove any of them, then press *Next*.

## Reviewing the tree

*Review* shows the tree the wizard would build, before any of it exists. Its columns say what
each item is and what it brings:

- the type it was given, and the file it came from
- its word count and its scene breaks
- how many comments and footnotes it carries
- the epigraph heading it, when the file had one
- what becomes of text the item's own type cannot hold

Change an item's type in its *Type* column. Untick *Import* to leave that item, and everything
under it, out.

## Setting whole heading levels

*Heading levels*, above the tree, says what a whole heading depth becomes. The depths your
files use are taken in order down Book, Part, Chapter, Scene, starting at Book.

The deepest depth gets a type that can hold prose. For chapter files that carry no book heading
of their own, *Add top level* inserts a Book above everything found.

Headings become Book, Part, Chapter or Scene, and stop there: nothing nests inside a scene. An
item can also be retyped to Note or Paratext, off that ladder. See
[the writing model](:help-writing-model) for what each type is.

## Choosing where it lands

On *Destination*, pick a binder or an item. The import goes inside what you chose if it is a
folder, and just after it otherwise.

A *Merge* step follows, but only when your files bring back items the project already holds and
at least one of them needs a decision. That is the round trip, and
[its own page](:help-round-trip) explains the choices.

## Finishing

*Import* writes everything in one step. A message then says how many items arrived and offers
*Undo* for a few seconds. If the write is refused, the wizard stays open with your plan intact.

## Comments and footnotes

Comments in a Word or OpenDocument file arrive as real Skribisto comments, on the words they
marked. A comment brings its author and its replies with it. See [comments](:help-comments).

Footnotes arrive from those two formats too, each with its own text, called from the same place
in the prose. Numbers are not carried.

A note the wizard cannot place is reported rather than invented.

## What the wizard reports

A strip under the tree carries the wizard's messages about the import.

- A file with no headings arrives as one item.
- A file that cannot be read, or whose format nothing recognizes, is skipped and named. The
  rest of the batch still comes in.
- An item can carry text its own type cannot hold, since a Book or a Part holds none. The *Its
  text* column then offers a choice. *Keep as paratext*, the default, gives that text a
  paratext of its own just inside the item; *Drop the text* leaves it behind.
